ADR-001 written to `ADR-001-notification-architecture.md`. The core reasoning:

**Redis Streams** — the decision hinges on two constraints that the team cannot negotiate around: the 6-person team has no infra engineer and no Kafka experience, and the ≤2-week time-to-value deadline is hard. Kafka's operational complexity (broker tuning, partition management, consumer rebalancing, schema registry) imposes a 3–4 week onboarding cost on a team learning it from scratch, while Redis Streams can ship a working async pipeline in one week using infrastructure already deployed and monitored.

The throughput argument reinforces rather than overrides this: at 500 req/s peak (2,000 events/s, projected to 5,000), Redis Streams has 25–50x headroom — Kafka's 100k+ msgs/s throughput is capacity paid for upfront that will not be needed. The single-node bottleneck (Redis's real ceiling at ~50k ops/s) becomes relevant only well past the 10x growth target.
